            "text": "When a careers adviser visited her school, a sixteen-year-old asked, half joking, whether it was even worth studying for a job that might not exist by the time she graduated university. The adviser, to her surprise, did not dismiss the question. She said that nobody could honestly promise any student that a given career would remain unchanged for forty years, and that the question itself, she added, was more useful than most of the ones she was usually asked.\n\nShe went on to explain that several major reports had claimed automation would eliminate a significant share of current jobs within two decades, though she cautioned that earlier predictions along similar lines had often proved too dramatic, too soon. What she stressed repeatedly was that the specific tasks within a job, rather than the job title itself, mattered most when judging how vulnerable a role might be.\n\nA student near the front asked whether this meant vocational training was now a safer choice than a traditional university degree. The adviser replied that she would not go that far; she conceded that some trades requiring physical dexterity and on-the-spot judgement had so far proved harder to automate than many office-based roles, but she insisted that no single path could be guaranteed immune indefinitely.\n\nLater, a parent at the same event wanted to know whether schools were doing enough to prepare students for jobs that, by the adviser's own account, might not yet have been invented. The adviser admitted that the honest answer was probably no, and that most curricula, including the one her own students were following, had been designed for a labour market that was changing faster than any official syllabus could realistically keep pace with.\n\nWhat she recommended, in the end, was not a single subject or qualification but a cluster of competencies: the ability to learn quickly, to communicate clearly, to collaborate with people from different backgrounds, and to adapt when an entire plan had to be abandoned. She asked the students to consider which of these they were actually practising, as opposed to merely being told, in an assembly much like this one, that they were important.\n\nShe added one further observation that several students later said they found more useful than anything else in the talk: that the workforce they would eventually enter was likely to reward people who treated their own education as unfinished, continuing to retrain and update their skills long after leaving school, rather than people who regarded a single diploma, however impressive, as a completed task.\n\nWalking home afterwards, the student who had asked the original question found herself less reassured than she had expected but, oddly, less anxious as well. Nobody had promised her a stable career waiting at the end of a fixed number of years in education. What she had been offered instead, she decided, was a more honest, if less comfortable, description of what she would actually need: not a single safe answer, but the capacity to keep finding new ones."
